Based on the screenshot, the feature’s name in the KML file is written as

```
<name>Andy&amp;apos;s</name>
```

When we call `readString()` on that node, the DOM has already turned `&amp;apos;` into the literal string `&apos;`, and since we never do any further “HTML‐entity decoding,” the map ends up showing “Andy&apos;s” instead of “Andy’s.” In other words, we need to catch HTML‐encoded entities (like `&apos;`, `&quot;`, `&lt;`, `&gt;`, even stray `&amp;`) and turn them into their real characters before we hand the name off to the text renderer.

The simplest place to do that is right after we pull `feature.get('name')` in `createFeatureStyleFunction`.  We just run a small `.replace()` chain to decode the common entities, and then go on as before.

Here is a single *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch in `src/ol/format/KML.js`:

```javascript
### src/ol/format/KML.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
      if (drawName) {
        name = /** @type {string} */ (feature.get('name'));
        drawName = drawName && !!name;
      }
=======
      if (drawName) {
        name = /** @type {string} */ (feature.get('name'));
        // decode HTML entities in the name so "&apos;" → "'" etc.
        name = name.replace(/&apos;/g, "'")
                   .replace(/&quot;/g, '"')
                   .replace(/&lt;/g, '<')
                   .replace(/&gt;/g, '>')
                   .replace(/&amp;/g, '&');
        drawName = drawName && !!name;
      }
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With this in place, a KML `<name>` of `Andy&amp;apos;s` will correctly display as “Andy’s.”
